# Artifact Status

## Purpose

This repository is the research prototype for **K2O: Precise Key-to-Offset
Learned Indexing for Read-Efficient LSM-Tree KV Stores**. It is intended for
artifact inspection, compilation, and experimental evaluation.

## Included Implementations

| Method | Location | Status |
|---|---|---|
| K2O | `K2O/` | Prototype implementation included |
| GoogleLI | `Baselines/rocksdb-google/` | Baseline implementation included |
| LeaderKV | `Baselines/rocksdb-leader/` | Baseline implementation included |

Each implementation is based on RocksDB and can be compiled independently.
The repository also includes YCSB workload configurations and benchmark code
for random-read and mixed-workload evaluation.

## Implemented Scope

The artifact includes:

- the K2O learned-index implementation integrated with RocksDB;
- the GoogleLI and LeaderKV comparison implementations;
- `db_bench`-based random fill/read evaluation;
- YCSB workload configurations;
- configuration paths for Wikipedia and Facebook key workloads;
- logging and performance-statistics collection code.

## Current Limitations

- This is a research prototype and has not been validated for production use.
- The artifact is intended for Linux environments.
- No prebuilt binaries are provided; each method must be compiled locally.
- Database and output paths are machine-specific and must be configured before
  execution.
- Full-scale defaults use 64 million load operations and 10 million run
  operations, so execution time and storage consumption can be substantial.
- The helper scripts may clear the Linux page cache and therefore require
  appropriate `sudo` permission.
- Dataset-specific Wikipedia and Facebook experiments require the corresponding
  input data to be placed at the configured workload path.
- Performance values depend on CPU, memory, storage device, compiler, operating
  system, and cache state; exact numerical results are not expected to be
  hardware-independent.

## Expected Outputs

A completed benchmark run may produce:

- `log.txt` containing the complete `db_bench` output;
- files named `{benchmark}_{iops}.txt` containing extracted throughput;
- `{benchmark}_{li_tag}_stats_{suffix}.json` containing trace statistics;
- `{benchmark}_{li_tag}_io_counts_{suffix}.json` containing I/O counts.

See `INSTALL` for build and smoke-test instructions and `README.md` for the
full experimental configuration.
